  "text": "Certainly! Let's break down CAC and LTV using a simple analogy.\n\nImagine you're running a small lemonade stand:\n\n*   **CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost):** This is how much money you spend to get **one new customer** to buy your lemonade. For your stand, this might include the cost of the colorful sign you made, the flyers you handed out, or even the free sample you gave to entice someone. If you spent $10 on signs and flyers and got 5 new customers, your CAC would be $2 per customer ($10 / 5 customers).\n\n*   **LTV (Lifetime Value):** This is the total amount of money a **single customer** is expected to spend at your lemonade stand over the entire time they are your customer. If a customer buys a $1 lemonade every day for a week, their LTV for that week would be $7. If they only buy one $1 lemonade and never return, their LTV is just $1.\n\n**Why are they important together?**\n\nBusinesses want their **LTV to be much higher than their CAC**. It's like saying, \"I'm willing to spend $2 to get a new customer, but only if that customer is going to spend $7 or more on my lemonade over time.\" If your CAC is higher than your LTV (e.g., you spend $5 to get a customer who only buys $2 worth of lemonade), you're losing money!\n\nThese metrics help businesses understand if their marketing and sales efforts are profitable and sustainable.",
